    "case_id": "368",
    "case_summary": "A man in his 50s presented with a 12-year history of itchy eruptions over the whole body. Multiple nodules appeared on the upper back 1 year previously and gradually increased in number and size. The patient was otherwise healthy and denied a family history of inherited ichthyosis or atopy.\n\nPhysical examination revealed extensive, dry scaly patches on the trunk, arms, and legs. Several 0.5- to 1-cm, dome-shaped, red papules and nodules were found on the upper back. Mildly enlarged lymph nodes were observed on the cervical, axillary, and inguinal regions.\n\n- Laboratory Tests:\n  - Routine blood tests, biochemistry analyses, and peripheral blood flow cytometry tests were within normal limits\n  - No atypical cells were found in the peripheral blood\n  - DNA analysis from paraffin sections showed clonal amplification product for the γ-chain of the T-cell receptor\n\n- Histopathology:\n  - Biopsy from back nodules showed:\n    - Atrophy of the epidermis with focal orthokeratosis\n    - Extensive, dense infiltrate of lymphocytes in the entire dermis\n    - Small to medium-sized, atypical lymphocytes with focal areas of epidermotropism\n  - Immunohistochemical staining showed:\n    - Positive for CD2, CD3, CD4, and CD45RO\n    - Few small reactive CD8+ T cells and CD7+ T cells in dermis\n\n- Images:\n  Title: Clinical and Histopathologic Images\n  Description: Clinical images showing multiple dome-shaped nodules on the back and extensive, dry scaly patches on the leg. Histopathologic images showing small to medium-sized atypical cells, focal areas of epidermotropism, and CD4 on immunohistochemical analysis",
